WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.160
Embedded security begins with a realistic
threat model.

2
00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:07.008
Before choosing controls, the team should
understand what the device protects,

3
00:00:07.088 --> 00:00:10.191
what it controls, what data it stores,
what interfaces it exposes,

4
00:00:10.271 --> 00:00:13.098
and how it is manufactured, updated,
serviced, and retired.

5
00:00:13.178 --> 00:00:16.447
A device may include local physical
access, maintenance connectors,

6
00:00:16.527 --> 00:00:20.018
serial ports, wireless interfaces, cloud
connections, removable media,

7
00:00:20.098 --> 00:00:23.368
service commands, manufacturing
credentials, field-update paths,

8
00:00:23.448 --> 00:00:24.889
and safety-critical outputs.

9
00:00:24.969 --> 00:00:27.074
Rust helps reduce some implementation
risk,

10
00:00:27.154 --> 00:00:30.923
but it does not choose the architecture or
define which interfaces should exist.

11
00:00:31.003 --> 00:00:33.330
Physical access assumptions need to be
explicit.

12
00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:36.236
Some products assume devices are in
controlled facilities.

13
00:00:36.316 --> 00:00:40.251
Others assume they may be handled by
customers, technicians, or untrusted parties.

14
00:00:40.331 --> 00:00:43.545
The design should state what physical
access means for debug policy,

15
00:00:43.625 --> 00:00:46.507
key storage, update behavior, data
retention, and recovery.

16
00:00:46.587 --> 00:00:49.967
A useful threat model turns assumptions
into reviewable requirements.

17
00:00:50.047 --> 00:00:53.760
Boot, update, debug, configuration,
storage, communications, diagnostics,

18
00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:57.220
and recovery behavior should each have
requirements that can be tested.

19
00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.683
The point is not to predict every possible
failure;

20
00:00:59.763 --> 00:01:03.642
it is to make important assumptions
visible before firmware design hardens around

21
00:01:03.722 --> 00:01:03.999
them.

22
00:01:04.079 --> 00:01:07.127
Embedded systems also blend safety,
reliability, and security.

23
00:01:07.207 --> 00:01:09.645
A malformed message, resource leak, failed
update,

24
00:01:09.725 --> 00:01:13.327
or unclear recovery path can become a
security concern when it affects device

25
00:01:13.407 --> 00:01:14.959
availability or safe operation.

26
00:01:15.039 --> 00:01:18.807
Good threat modeling keeps these concerns
connected instead of treating them as

27
00:01:18.887 --> 00:01:19.940
separate checklists.
